A||||8||lennyb4||On or Off ?? Whats better??||||6||
Z||000000||lennyb4||07-07-2000||02:55 PM||||Hey guys. I have decided to bite the bullet and try to paint my car myself. I know i can do it because i painted the quarters i got from dave awhile ago and they turned out great. Heres my question. I was planning on pulling off the entire body to redo (sand and re-paint) the space frame. Is it better to sand,repair,paint, and clear coat all the panels off the car then reassemble or reassemble and then paint. Im just worried that if i do them all seperately that they wont turn out the same (color wise). Any info would greatly be appreciated.....TIA||||reg||6
Z||000001||dman||07-07-2000||03:17 PM||||Hi lennyb4. I too would be interested in hearing some answers to your question. But I'd like clarify the question a bit.<P>If the panels are painted off of the car all in one sitting then everything should come out just great.<P>But if the panels are painted seperately, on weekends for example. And it takes three or four weekends. What potential problems or gotchas should one look out for?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jlruiz67||07-07-2000||08:43 PM||||If you know how much paint it will take to paint the car in parts you can mix the paint and have extra and you should be ok as far as paiting the parts on weekends. you should not wait a long time in case you face paint degradation but it should be ok. Also if you do this you have the problem of nicks and scratches while fitting the car. I would just prime everything and assemble and then paint. Just my 2cents||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||07-07-2000||09:04 PM||||If you disassemble the body to paint it, make sure you paint each panel just as it will go on the car.  (i.e.  the hood needs to be horizontal, the doors need to be vertical, etc)<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||07-07-2000||09:54 PM||||Come out better with panels off...no overspray from a bad tape job.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gokart||07-07-2000||10:06 PM||||I agree with Old Lar, but I was told that painting separate pieces over time will reveal different shades cause you can't ever mix the EXACT same color paint twice!<P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierodave||07-08-2000||01:46 AM||||Lenny you can panel paint the car with no problems at all!  <BR>just buy a gallon and thin it per cans instructions<BR>count the number of coats you use that way later if you need to redo a panel it will match!<BR>leave the quarter section and roof on the car for painting. after painting the parts put the doors back on first then the fenders, cover etc<BR>if there is no metallic's in the paint there is no need to worry about how or what angle the parts are at when you paint them!  the metallic's in a paint job need to "Lay out"  not with a solid color! (black,white,red,yellow,blue)<BR>the med red needs the parts angled the way they would be on the car along with the silver too!  If you base clear it,  it's a walk in the park! (the base coat that is)<BR>give me a call I'll walk you through it<BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||07-08-2000||11:37 PM||||As far as personal opinions go, I would rather paint the panels while on the car.  I can see taking them all off when going to another paint shade than original, but why mess with it when you go to the same color?<P>The way I see it is that there are a lot more possibilities of hurting the paint when putting the panels back on than when they are already there.  Of course, I have been known to have a different opinion the rest also, so use this as a personal opinion only.<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>86 Black 3.1 GT<BR>87 Gold GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierodave||07-09-2000||06:20 AM||||Mike <BR>I think he would like the car to look the way it did new from the factory! <P>that's how it was done in the factory and there wasn't to much trouble with hurting the paint,  <BR>If your doing a show quality paint job, You should try to duplicate the process as close to the way the factory did it as possible, I have done a couple Old mopar restos (roadrunner & superbee's)  That sold for over $17,000.00 each, and we tried to duplicte the factory paint and assembly methods.<P>I would rather spend the  extra time in making sure I didn't scratch the paint in reassembly, then to try to detail the over spray off the nice new black paint on the space frame! and if you scratch a panel putting it on, just respray it!<P>but I agree with you if your doing a everyday driver and staying with the same color, you will just have to spend tons of time masking up! and then the detail work, <BR>I think it's easier to remove and repaint if you want a nice looking factory fresh Job!<BR>FD<P>------------------<P>Fiero Cars & Parts Located in Mosinee,Wi Website: <A HREF="http://www.Fierodave.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.Fierodave.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fierodave (edited 07-09-2000).]||||reg||1
